  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Wells Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Address:</b> Lockhart<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Caldwell]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.5767398529263,29.8268091449011,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Andrew A. Rasmussen</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[Main &amp; 15th St., Port O'Connor<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Port O'Connor<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Calhoun<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> A native of Denmark, Andrew Rasmussen (1858-1933) came to the United States at the age of 13 and began a maritime career that lasted for 60 years. Following the shipwreck of his schooner, the "Dorio and Doria," in 1885, Rasmussen joined Calhoun County's Saluria Life Saving Service, which had rescued his crew. By 1889, he was placed in charge of the station, which later became part of the U.S Coast Guard. Capt. Rasmussen and his wife, Theresa Amelia (Smith), built a home at this site in 1911. They are buried in the Port Lavaca Cemetery. (1985)]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-96.418424,28.441941,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Calhoun County</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[1.2 mi. W Port Lavaca, US 87-State Hwy 35<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Port Lavaca<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Calhoun<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Calhoun County, Formed from Victoria, Jackson, and Matagorda Counties, Created April 4, 1846, Organized July 13, 1846, Named in Honor of Joyn Caldwell Calhoun, 1782-1850, a Distinguished Southern Statesman, A Member of Congress and of the Cabinet, Vice-President of the United States, 1825-1832, Port Lavaca, The County Seat]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-96.7222266714817,28.6226078052326,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
