  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Community of Pilgrim</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[from Gonzales, take Hwy. 97 S about 2.25 miles, then take FM 1116 south about 15.2 miles.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Pilgrim<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Named in honor of Thomas J. Pilgrim (1804-1877), noted pioneer educator. Born in Connecticut, he came in 1828 to Texas, where in 1829 he organized a school at San Felipe, in Stephen F. Austin's Colony. Also started first Sunday School in Texas. In 1838 he received a land grant of 1,476 acres (including this site) from Texas Republic. Settled in Gonzales in 1840; started a Sunday School and was superintendent until 1871. Community became a prominent trading post in early 1840s and a hideout of famous gunman John Wesley Hardin in 1870s.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.521397,29.296014,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Community of Wrightsboro</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[FM 108,Wrightsboro<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Wrightsboro<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Founded in 1851 by L. C. "Jack" Wright. Fellow settlers with a sense of humor gave town name because Wright's burro (donkey) roamed the area at will. A native of Alabama, Wright (1820-1905) was a noted marksman. He served in Confederate Army in Civil War (1861-65), then returned to stock raising here. Was also a county commissioner 10 years. At peak, Wrightsboro boasted many stores and a solid grain and cotton-based economy. Grew to be trade and social center. Local Masonic Lodge, Shuler No. 317, has operated from 1869 to 1915 and 1920 to the present.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.56478,29.373128,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>David L. Kokernot</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[(private propety) from Gonzales, take US 90-A SE 13 mile to FM 443; go South about 1.5 mile<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Star and Wreath A soldier, officer, scout and courier in the Texas War for Independence, 1835-36. Erected by the State of Texas, 1962]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.2620789546238,29.4410194693155,0</coordinates></Point>
