  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Salt Flats of Pilgrim Lake</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[from Gonzales, take SH 97 S about 2.25 miles to FM 1116 South. Follow about 13.75 miles S. to roadside area.<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Long before the arrival of Spaniards or Anglo-Americans, this natural salt source was probably utilized by native Indians. Although not produced in great quantities, salt was processed here by pioneers in the 1800s. In damp weather, salty "dew" forms on the flats.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.506043,29.317518,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Site of Hopkinsville</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[FM 196, 5 miles North of Welder.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Once a thriving pioneer community. Founded by D. S. Hopkins (1819-1917), farmer who settled here in 1852. Located in farm-ranch area. Herds started here, bound for Chisholm trail. Abandoned 1873 when the citizens moved south and founded town of Waelder on newly built railroad.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.3117147024024,29.7572251551406,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>St. James Missionary Baptist Church</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[7231 FM 532, Gonzalez<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 7231 FM 532, Gonzales<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Gonzales<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Founded in 1867 on Brasco Lake in nearby Dilworth, Gonzales County, St. James Missionary Baptist Church was a staple of the early Peach Creek Community. Under the leadership of the Reverend Boyd James (1826-1916), the congregation of local formerly enslaved people worshipped under a brush arbor structure until 1879 when they began meeting at a newly-built school. In 1892, St. James bought land of their own. A church was built and rebuilt in 1962. The land has been used for church and community events such as Juneteenth celebrations. For more than 150 years, St. James has been a place of hope, enriching the community through veterans services, youth groups and spiritual guidance. (2022)]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.309728,29.550665,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Women of the Confederacy</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
