  <Placemark>
    <name>City View School</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[1023 City View Drive (inside school) Wichita Falls<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 1023 City View Dr., Wichita Falls<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Wichita<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Created in 1889 as Common School District No. 8, City View School was renamed by its students because of its original site (ca. 2 miles northwest) which featured a commanding view of the surrounding city. The school was relocated in 1926 to this site, in a building nicknamed "The Alamo" because of its mission revival architecture. It was replaced by a new facility in 1960. Experiencing steady growth, City View School was officially designated an independent school district in 1961. A number of buildings have been added to the campus over the years. (1990)]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-98.5528886661333,33.9347887221346,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Dr. Samuel Hiatt Burnside, Mary Margaret Grice Burnside</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[Riverside Cemetery 1810 5th Street, Wichita Falls from Main gate, take drive straight about .1 mile.  Turn right, about 100feet to graves on south side of drive<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 1810 5th St., Wichita Falls<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Wichita<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Kentucky native Samuel H. Burnside, an 1882 graduate of the University of Pennsylvania, opened a medical practice in Wichita Falls in 1883. Mary Margaret Grice, a native of Philadelphia, moved to Wichita Falls in 1885 at the invitation of her uncle, George Fox, and was treated for tuberculosis by Dr. Burnside. A romance ensued, and the two were married in 1887. Mary purchased land on Seventh Street where the couple built a large home, which also housed the doctor's office. Dr. Burnside built a hospital, the Wichita Falls Sanitarium, next door to the house in 1907. Well respected in medical circles, Dr. Burnside was a renowned physician who also served as chief surgeon for two railroad companies and medical examiner for twenty-four insurance companies. He performed innovative surgeries and often treated Comanche Indians from the nearby reservations in Oklahoma. Mary Burnside was a leader in local and state civic organizations, including the daughters of the American Revolution and the Texas Federation of Women's Clubs. Both Dr. and Mrs. Burnside were active in the First Presbyterian Church. They were the parents of five children, two of whom lived to adulthood. (1994)]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-98.5071018067521,33.9117218258683,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>George "Tex" Barringer</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[marker pending<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 1400 9th St, Wichita Falls<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Wichita<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> marker pending]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-98.498543,33.906866,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Hodges-Hardy House</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[1100 Travis, Wichita Falls<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 1100 Travis, Wichita Falls<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Wichita<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Local builders W. Lee Moore and James A. Richolt constructed this residence in 1893-95 for Samuel H. Hodges, an attorney and real estate investor. The late Victorian style house has a wraparound porch and Eastlake detailing. Hodges sold the property in 1910 to Dan Hardy, a merchant, oilman, and landowner. Hardy and his wife Myrtle were prominent philthropists and civic leaders. The house remained in their family for over 70 years. Recorded Texas Historic Landmark - 1984.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-98.4919310233118,33.9074395139063,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
